2. THE READING RULE (the task's, verbatim): a session is a BAD PROBE if its tbr != 60. THE MECHANISM REPRODUCES if every
   bad-probe session in A has output rate = tbr and drops ~ (60 - tbr) x 60 +/- 20 %. THE FIX WORKS if every bad-probe
   session in B has output rate 60 and drops <= 2 in the minute. SAMPLED ENOUGH if arm B holds >= 3 bad-probe sessions;
   if not after 24, continue to 48 per arm (§1's extension); if still < 3, INCONCLUSIVE (the probe did not misread
   tonight). The bad-probe rate per arm is reported either way.

3. THE BAR -- ACCEPTED only if ALL hold: sampled enough; the mechanism reproduces in A; the fix works in B; the live
   session's companion-side metrics are host-clean: RetroArch >= 59.9, and host CPU within E1's band (+/- 2 points) of
   E4-S1's SNES 7.4 % plus the bench's own cost, reported separately. THE BENCH'S OWN COST (host %, over the live
   window) = the bench sessions' CPU seconds inside the window / (window s x 12 cores) x 100, plus Xorg's CPU above
   E4-S1 SNES's (procs.xorg.cpu_pct 7.69 % of a core) / 12 -- the second x11grab's server-side cost; i.e. the gate is
   |host CPU - bench cost - 7.4| <= 2. AND §4 passes (the flag changes nothing for PS1; the flag applies to every
   x11grab session, so it is part of what an adoption would carry). REJECTED if any bad-probe B session drops > 2 or
   outputs != 60, or if §4 fails. Any other unmet condition (not sampled enough; the mechanism not reproducing in A; the
   live session not host-clean) = INCONCLUSIVE, the clause named. The link (loss, rendered fps) is REPORTED, NOT
   GATED; so is the companion's own encoder (no flag: its probe and drops are the fault itself, reported as a sample).

